  "sentence_claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S01",
      "text": "This chapter defines the methodology used to study closed-set visual question answering on GQA.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "literature_background"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:framing", "S1:primary_task"],
      "citation_keys": ["hudson2019gqa"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S02",
      "text": "The central problem is whether lightweight trainable heads can make effective use of frozen multimodal representations, while keeping development-set accuracy and measured efficiency, understood here as warm-serial latency and trainable parameter count, in view.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:framing", "S1:efficiency_boundary", "S4:efficiency_claim_boundary"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S03",
      "text": "Within the global pathway, fixed representations come from the separate image and text towers of a frozen CLIP checkpoint.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "literature_gap"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:frozen_lightweight", "S2:clip_global"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "gap_marker": "CITE-GAP clip_foundation"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S04",
      "text": "This broader use of frozen pretrained components is established in vision-language research, but here it is treated as a controlled design choice rather than a claim about any one architecture.",
      "claim_types": ["literature_background", "project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:frozen_lightweight"],
      "citation_keys": ["tsimpoukelli2021frozen"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S05",
      "text": "Four principles govern the experiments.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:frozen_lightweight", "S1:controls_replication", "S1:development_confirmatory_separation"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S06",
      "text": "First, representation encoders remain frozen, so comparisons concern the trainable interface or head.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:frozen_lightweight"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S07",
      "text": "Second, matched controls are used wherever an architectural or pretraining choice is isolated.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:controls_replication"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S08",
      "text": "Third, models are replicated across predefined seeds rather than interpreted from a single run.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:controls_replication"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S09",
      "text": "Finally, development evidence is kept separate from future confirmatory evaluation: it supports model development and analysis, but not a held-out generalisation claim.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_governance"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:development_confirmatory_separation"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S10",
      "text": "The formal research questions are to be stated in Chapter 1.",
      "claim_types": ["project_structure", "author_decision"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:chapter_organization"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "xref_marker": "XREF-PENDING: Chapter 1 RQ definitions",
      "author_decision_note": "Formal RQ wording was resolved by explicit human decision in this task's coordination request; the packet records the decision but is not cited as scientific evidence."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S11",
      "text": "As an organising map, RQ1 concerns global heads and representation or answer-space choices; RQ2 concerns token-level reasoning under data scaling; and RQ3 concerns frozen small-language-model interfaces and their bounded efficiency evidence.",
      "claim_types": ["project_structure", "author_decision"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:chapter_organization", "S1:efficiency_boundary"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.1-S12",
      "text": "Chapters 4-6 address these questions in turn, while Chapter 7 reports the efficiency analysis.",
      "claim_types": ["project_structure"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:chapter_organization", "S1:efficiency_boundary"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S01",
      "text": "The experimental task uses the balanced release of GQA, a benchmark designed around questions grounded in image content and compositional scene structure.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "literature_background"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:primary_task"],
      "citation_keys": ["hudson2019gqa"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S02",
      "text": "Each example supplies an image and a natural-language question, and the system maps that pair to one answer from a fixed vocabulary.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:primary_task"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S03",
      "text": "This is the primary task throughout the dissertation: discriminative closed-set VQA, rather than unrestricted language generation.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:primary_task"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S04",
      "text": "Closed-vocabulary classification has a long-standing role in VQA evaluation; the project vocabulary is constructed from the training pool only so development images cannot determine its support.",
      "claim_types": ["literature_background", "project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:vocabulary_construction"],
      "citation_keys": ["antol2015vqa", "jabri2016revisiting"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S05",
      "text": "Two later experiment families use bounded generation only as an evaluation readout.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:e8b_r2", "S1:e8b_r3", "S1:e9"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S06",
      "text": "In the answer-side language-model family (E8B, described in Section 3.7), the R2 readout decodes greedily under a prefix trie restricted to the closed answer vocabulary, while the R3 readout removes that restriction but stays greedy and stops at a fixed token cap.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:e8b_r2", "S1:e8b_r3", "S3:answer_side_interface"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S07",
      "text": "The compact vision-language models used for context (E9, defined in Section 3.7) are likewise evaluated with a pinned greedy readout.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:e9"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S08",
      "text": "These exceptions test how an already defined representation or readout produces an answer; they do not extend the task to open-ended generation.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:e8b_r2", "S1:e8b_r3", "S1:e9", "S1:generation_exclusions"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S09",
      "text": "Sampling, temperature search and prompt search are excluded.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:generation_exclusions"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S10",
      "text": "Keeping this distinction explicit prevents a change in output mechanism from being mistaken for a change in the scientific task.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:primary_task", "S1:generation_exclusions"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S11",
      "text": "The same boundary applies to every classifier and readout family: the image-question input is fixed, answer production is bounded, and every trainable component is fitted to a fixed answer set defined for that experiment.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:primary_task", "S1:generation_exclusions", "S1:vocabulary_construction", "S2:top1000_extension", "S3:answer_side_interface"],
      "canonical_source_refs": ["e3_vocab1000_report"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S12",
      "text": "The training objective differs by family: the classifier heads and the language-model-free readout use a cross-entropy loss over the closed answer vocabulary, whereas the answer-side language-model readouts are trained with a teacher-forced token-level loss on the answer tokens (each answer token is predicted from the preceding gold tokens), with decoding bounded at evaluation as described above.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:primary_task", "S3:answer_side_interface"],
      "canonical_source_refs": ["classifier_training_source", "e8b_b1_objective", "e8b_b2_objective", "e8b_b3_objective", "e10_b4_objective", "e10_b4r_objective"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.2-S13",
      "text": "The scoring rules and evaluation denominators are defined in Section 3.5.",
      "claim_types": ["cross_reference", "project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:primary_task"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S01",
      "text": "The GQA data were partitioned at image level so that questions associated with one image cannot be split between development and the training pool.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "literature_context"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:image_disjointness"],
      "citation_keys": ["hudson2019gqa"],
      "claim_components": {
        "literature_context": "GQA dataset identity is supported by hudson2019gqa.",
        "project_methodology": "Image-level project partition construction is supported by S1:image_disjointness and its v2_00 sources."
      }
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S02",
      "text": "As summarised in Table 3.1, the complete development partition contains 10,004 raw questions on 777 images.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_numeric"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:development_partition"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "numeric_tokens": ["10,004", "777"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S03",
      "text": "Applying the fixed-vocabulary filter retains 7,714 questions represented across 768 images.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_numeric"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:development_partition", "S1:development_manifest_images"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "numeric_tokens": ["7,714", "768"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S04",
      "text": "The two image counts therefore describe different scopes: 777 is the complete partition, whereas 768 counts images represented by retained in-vocabulary rows.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_numeric"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:development_partition", "S1:development_manifest_images"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "numeric_tokens": ["777", "768"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "TAB-3.1-CAPTION",
      "text": "Data partitions and nested training scales. Image counts distinguish complete-partition images from images represented by retained rows; question counts distinguish eligible pool rows from selected training-prefix rows.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:development_partition", "S1:development_manifest_images", "S1:training_pool", "S1:nested_scales"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S05",
      "text": "The remaining training partition contains 71,363 images, while the vocabulary rule makes 724,074 questions eligible for selection.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_numeric"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:training_pool"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "numeric_tokens": ["71,363", "724,074"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S06",
      "text": "Three training manifests take strict row-for-row prefixes of this shared question order: 40k is contained in 100k, which is contained in 250k.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_numeric"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:nested_scales"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "numeric_tokens": ["40k", "100k", "250k"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S07",
      "text": "Their represented-image counts are 27,622, 46,158 and 61,859 respectively.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_numeric"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:nested_scales"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "numeric_tokens": ["27,622", "46,158", "61,859"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S08",
      "text": "Figure 3.1 shows this nesting together with the image-disjoint boundaries.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "cross_reference"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:nested_scales", "S1:image_disjointness"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "FIG-3.1-CAPTION",
      "text": "Image-disjoint data regions and row-for-row nesting of the training-question prefixes. The clean-test region is shown only at structural level; its targets remain embargoed.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:nested_scales", "S1:image_disjointness", "S1:clean_test_structure"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S09",
      "text": "This is a question-level scaling design.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:scaling_property"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S10",
      "text": "Moving to a larger prefix adds images that were not represented earlier, but can also add further questions for images already present.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_limitation"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:scaling_property"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S11",
      "text": "The manifests cannot separate these two effects, so Chapter 8 treats this as a design limitation rather than attributing scaling effects to image count alone.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_limitation", "cross_reference"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:scaling_property"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S12",
      "text": "The clean-test region is image-disjoint from both development and training.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:image_disjointness"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S13",
      "text": "Only its structural counts are reported: 8,013 questions on 972 images.",
      "claim_types": ["project_methodology", "project_numeric"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:clean_test_structure"],
      "citation_keys": [],
      "numeric_tokens": ["8,013", "972"]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S14",
      "text": "Targets remain embargoed until the model list is frozen, as set out in Section 3.12.",
      "claim_types": ["project_governance", "cross_reference"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:clean_test_structure", "S1:development_confirmatory_separation"],
      "citation_keys": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "3.3-S15",
      "text": "No clean-test result exists yet, and none has informed the methods or development choices reported here.",
      "claim_types": ["project_governance"],
      "project_fact_refs": ["S1:development_confirmatory_separation"],
      "canonical_source_refs": ["pre_f1_lifecycle"],
      "citation_keys": []
    }
  ],
